    add('| Given N12 class size | Class ID | Observed worst active label count | Binary decision-tree lower bound |');add('|---:|---:|---:|---:|')
    for c in p['classes']:add(f"| {len(c['members'])} | {c['class_id']} | {max(rows[cid,'active']['labels_used'] for cid in c['members'])} | {math.ceil(math.log2(len(c['members'])))} |")
    add();add('Descriptively, each class reaches the information-theoretic minimum worst-case depth: a binary tree distinguishing K candidates needs at least ceil(log2 K) levels. This bound is conditional on the supplied candidate set and noiseless binary answers; it is not a guarantee for larger classes or new rule families.');add()
    add('For every step, the selector scans all 73,712 ordinary-queen N13 boards except previously queried boards. It minimizes the absolute difference between the number of positive and negative candidate predictions, then chooses the lexicographically first row-to-column vector. The selector runs in a separate process and receives only the candidate prediction matrix, its candidate-ID ordering, the current compatible candidates, and earlier query IDs. It has no true-model argument or oracle interface. This is source-level interface isolation, not an adversarial operating-system sandbox.');add()
    add('Every selected query and its candidate predictions are written, flushed and fsynced before the evaluator calls the hidden-truth oracle. The query receipt is made read-only; the subsequent answer receipt references its hash and records both UTC and monotonic timestamps. The public audit independently reconstructs the minimum balance and full lexicographic tie set, verifies the chosen board and label, and retains every compatible candidate. All 102 selected queries and answers are public.');add()
    add(f"The random control freezes a full four-board order before any answer for each truth, using NumPy default_rng with seed 2026118000 + candidate ID. It samples uniformly without replacement from the full ordinary-queen universe and would also stop early at uniqueness. Only {sum(r['positive_labels'] for r in control)} of its 68 labels was positive; for true candidate 162 that positive removed candidate 157 but left four candidates. The other sixteen random runs retained their full starting class. This is one predeclared seed per truth, so 0/17 is a descriptive realized outcome, not an estimate of a universal random-query failure rate. The earlier positive-only studies use a different oracle and cannot be compared as equal-cost supervision.");add()
    add('H3ba had already established that the complete N13 populations distinguish these models. H3bb therefore tests a query strategy on known populations, not an unseen-size or unseen-model prediction. It adds no N14 census, no learned CRAM optimizer result, and no search-throughput comparison. The initial complete N12 class remains a strong assumption; the next useful test removes it while preserving the same finite candidate vocabulary.');add()
    add(f"The monitored whole study used **{s['aggregate_CPU_seconds']:.6f} aggregate CPU seconds / {s['wall_seconds']:.6f} wall seconds**, under frozen 120 CPU / 180 wall second limits; zero GPU work. Exact boolean membership arrays let the selector scan all boards without repeated rule enumeration. The budget includes independent literal enumeration of all seventeen N13 populations, ordinary-universe parity, and selector-process overhead. The separate public audit took {s['audit']['elapsed_seconds']:.6f} wall seconds and passed {s['audit']['checks']} checks. It re-enumerates all seventeen N12 source and N13 target populations, validates the supplied-class memberships, and independently replays every active and random step. The full 1,541-model N12 partition is hash-pinned from the independently audited H3ba study; H3bb takes that class as given rather than rediscovering it.");add()
